rd k+1 only ever faces an opponent card that has <i>already missed</i> your card k. Laying red right after red is a wasted attack, because their card already dodged red. Never lay the same colour twice in a row. “Common colours first” does the opposite and wins only <b>2%</b> against random."],["Count cards","Every card you see is information. The Card counter remembers revealed cards and re-optimises each set, winning <b>77%</b> against random and <b>60%</b> against the same optimiser without memory."],["Blackjack-style counting: two numbers are enough","With simple ordering, a running count of white and red cards does as well as perfect memory (51% head to head). A single white count adds almost nothing on its own (52% against plain zig-zag)."],["Equal players can play forever","Two deterministic or equally greedy players can fall into a ping-pong: short sets move a card or two back and forth, and the card counts stop moving. A finite deterministic system must eventually cycle, as in the card game War. In the tournament such games are stopped at 1,000 sets and count as draws."],["Bigger decks reward skill","Card counter vs random wins <b>64%</b> with 10 cards but <b>82%</b> with 80. More sets means a small per-set edge compounds, while random games grow like N² (a random walk)."],["Random play is a fair game","If nobody thinks, your chance of winning is almost exactly your share of the cards (a martingale, like gambler's ruin). A 20–20 game lasts about <b>30</b> sets on average."],["The deck changes the odds","Two random cards match 22% of the time in a sticker deck of famous teams, but only 18% across all 20,000+ clubs. It's 27% for Brazil’s Série A and 21% for Japan’s J1. Famous clubs wear white more often, so their cards match more."]],"vsRandom":{"Sticker deck (default)":{"pMatch":0.21676216888159536,"keep":{"win":0.5033333333333333,"draw":0,"sets":31.316666666666666},"colourful":{"win":0.4766666666666667,"draw":0,"sets":34.41},"plain":{"win":0.466666666666666